## Who gets a Blue Badge automatically

In England you usually qualify **automatically** if you:

- get the **higher rate mobility part of DLA**
- get PIP and scored **8 points or more** in the **moving around** activity
- get PIP and scored **10 points** in the **planning and following journeys** activity because you cannot go on any journey due to **overwhelming psychological distress**
- are registered **severely sight impaired** (blind)
- get a **War Pensioners' Mobility Supplement**
- get certain payments under the **Armed Forces Compensation Scheme**

## Who else can apply

You may still get a Blue Badge, after your council assesses you, if you:

- have **great difficulty walking**, or cannot walk at all
- have a **hidden disability**, such as autism, dementia or a mental health condition, and journeys cause you **very considerable difficulty**, **overwhelming distress**, or put you or others at **risk**
- have a **severe disability in both arms** and drive regularly but cannot use parking meters
- are a parent of a child **under 3** who needs bulky medical equipment or must stay near a vehicle for treatment

## How to apply

Apply online through **GOV.UK**, which passes your application to your council. In England, councils can charge **up to £10**. In Wales, Blue Badges are **free**. A badge usually lasts **up to 3 years**.

## Where you can park

Rules can vary by council, but usually you can:

- park **free** in on-street pay and display bays and at meters
- park on **single or double yellow lines** for **up to 3 hours** in England and Wales, unless there is a loading ban
- use **disabled parking bays**

Always display your badge and the parking clock correctly. Private car parks, such as supermarkets and hospitals, set their own rules.

In **London**, Blue Badge holders can register for a discount on the **Congestion Charge**. Some central London boroughs run their own schemes.

{% callout type="warn" title="Using a badge correctly" %}
The badge is for the disabled person. It can only be used when they are travelling in the car, or when the driver is collecting or dropping them off. Misuse can lead to a fine of up to £1,000.
{% /callout %}
